data.content:
## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

**Pattern identified:** Timeout enforcement — aborting a thread that exceeds a time limit.

**Why it breaks:** `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ (SYSLIB0006). `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also removed.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

If the `work` delegate supports cancellation (preferred), pass the token through:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|-----------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | Cooperative cancellation only works if `work` checks the token. Long-running loops must call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints. |
| **Blocking calls ignore the token** | `Thread.Sleep`, synchronous I/O won't respond to cancellation. Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or async I/O instead. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. Leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch at the orchestration boundary (as shown). Let it propagate elsewhere. |
| **Truly uncooperative code** | If you cannot modify `work` to check the token (e.g., third-party lib), the work will continue running after timeout. For hard kills, move the work to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill()`. |

---

### What was removed

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Replacement |
|--------------------|-------------------|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `CancellationTokenSource(timeout)` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested`, decide whether to continue |

The fundamental shift: cancellation is now **cooperative** — the running code must voluntarily check and respond to the token. There is no way to forcibly inject an exception into a managed thread in modern .NET.
